5, -0.0009126663208007812, 0.008697509765625, 0.00994873046875, 0.00788116455078125, 0.0189208984375, -0.025848388671875, 0.0097808837890625, 0.0287322998046875, 0.0211944580078125, -0.00597381591796875, -0.0159912109375, 0.00325775146484375, -0.011138916015625, 0.0204620361328125, -0.0254669189453125, 0.003681182861328125, -0.01132965087890625, -0.0338134765625, -0.00734710693359375, -0.0019855499267578125, 0.002086639404296875, 0.0017690658569335938, 0.06658935546875, 0.0081939697265625, 0.01073455810546875, -0.02581787109375, -0.006877899169921875, 0.0013666152954101562, 0.01262664794921875, 0.007354736328125, 0.0027332305908203125, -0.0244598388671875, 0.0079803466796875, -0.0179901123046875, -0.00518798828125, -0.01397705078125, -0.029022216796875, 0.02777099609375, -0.034820556640625, -0.0110015869140625, -0.032501220703125, -0.01580810546875, 0.0212554931640625, -0.024871826171875, -0.00688934326171875, -0.0005869865417480469], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. Despite promising results in recent literature, the reliability and robustness\u2014hereafter referred to as \"fragility,\" meaning susceptibility to performance degradation under varying conditions\u2014of these methods have not been thoroughly examined.\n\nMethods\n\nWe conduct a re-evaluation of two representative memory-based self-improving agents: Agent A (a retrieval-augmented language model) and Agent B (a memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent). Both agents utilize a textual memory bank to store task solutions and feedback. Our evaluation focuses on a suite of multi-step reasoning tasks in the ALFWorld and BabyAI environments, which require agents to solve sequential challenges in simulated household and navigation settings, respectively. Performance is measured by task completion rate and average reward. To ensure transparency and reproducibility, we report results over five independent runs per configuration.\n\nOur evaluation is comprehensive in two respects: (1) we assess both agents across two distinct environments and multiple task types, and (2) we systematically vary experimental conditions by shuffling task order and repeating runs to quantify variance. Baseline comparisons are made against agents without memory augmentation.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation exhibits high variance in complex, multi-step environments: for example, Agent A's task completion rate on ALFWorld ranged from 42% to 61% across runs (standard deviation: 7.8%), and variance increased further when the self-improving loop was enabled. Second, agent improvement is highly sensitive to task order; randomizing task sequences led to performance drops of up to 18% compared to the default curriculum order. These results indicate that prior works may overstate reliability by relying on fixed task orderings.\n\nTo investigate the source of this fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks by sampling stored entries and categorizing them based on specificity, relevance, and completeness. We observed that ambiguous or underspecified task descriptions and sparse environment feedback often led to ineffective memory entries, which in turn degraded agent performance. Based on these observations, we hypothesized that underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to fragility.\n\nTo validate this hypothesis, we augmented the memory construction process with detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback. Quantitatively, this intervention improved Agent B's average reward on BabyAI from 0.54 (\u00b10.09) to 0.67 (\u00b10.06), a statistically significant difference (p < 0.05, paired t-test). However, even with enhanced specification, performance remained below the baseline achieved with the default task order, indicating persistent gaps.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe remaining performance gaps\u2014up to 12% lower completion rates compared to the best-case curriculum\u2014suggest that factors beyond underspecification, such as memory retrieval errors or compounding noise in the self-improving loop, contribute to fragility. These gaps were measured by comparing the best and worst performing runs under different task orderings and memory augmentation strategies.\n\nRecommendations\n\nTo advance the field, we recommend the following concrete guidelines for evaluating memory-based self-improving agents: (1) report results over multiple independent runs and randomized task orders to capture variance and avoid curriculum bias; (2) provide detailed descriptions of tasks, environments, and evaluation metrics; (3) include ablation studies isolating the effects of memory specification and feedback; and (4) design interfaces that allow human reviewers to inspect and edit memory entries, enabling oversight and correction of agent failures.\n\nConclusion\n\nOur study highlights the fragility of current memory-based self-improving agents and underscores the need for rigorous, transparent evaluation protocols. Addressing underspecification and enabling effective human oversight are critical steps toward building more reliable and robust self-improving systems."}}